# ============================ PAYROLL 3 ============================
LESSONS_P3 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The run: one document, one procedure", "11", "<p>This module runs the payroll. Modules 1 and 2 built the records; this module executes them monthly through one doctype — the <b>Payroll Entry</b> — wrapped in one written procedure. The module covers: the Payroll Entry form and its lifecycle, the pre-run checklist, the draft review, submission and the posting, the payment, the post-run close, and corrections.</p><p><b>What the Payroll Entry is.</b> One Payroll Entry document is one pay run for one period: it selects the employees, generates their Salary Slips as drafts, submits them in bulk, books the accrual Journal Entry, and anchors the bank payment. Everything the run does hangs off this one document, which is what makes the run auditable: the month's payroll has one reference, and every slip, posting, and payment traces to it.</p><p><b>The lifecycle, previewed.</b> The run moves through five stations, and the module's chapters follow them:</p><p>1. <b>Prepare</b> — the records brought current before the run touches anything: joiners assigned, leavers dated, leave recorded, changes previewed (Chapter 2).<br>2. <b>Generate</b> — the Payroll Entry created, employees pulled, draft slips created (Chapter 3).<br>3. <b>Review</b> — the drafts read against the checks before anything submits: the headcount reconciliation, the register comparison, the spot-checks (Chapter 4).<br>4. <b>Submit and post</b> — the slips submitted, the accrual Journal Entry booked, the posting tied (Chapter 5).<br>5. <b>Pay and close</b> — the bank payment under the separation, the register filed, the custody sweep (Chapter 6).</p><p><b>The timing frame.</b> The run is a calendar event with dated stations — for a 25th payday, a working shape is: records closed by the 20th, drafts generated and reviewed the 21st-22nd, submitted the 23rd, paid the 25th, closed by the 27th with the remittance calendar (module 2) taking over. The exact dates are the business's; the <b>order</b> is this module's and does not vary: nothing submits before the review, nothing pays before the posting ties, and nothing is closed before the register files.</p><p><b>The one-operator risk, named.</b> The run concentrates power: the person who prepares it should not be the person who releases its money (module 2's separation, applied to salaries), and the review stations exist precisely so that errors — and anything worse — must pass a read that is not the preparer's own. The checklist is not bureaucracy; it is the run's audit face.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapters 2-6 walk the five stations. Chapter 7: corrections and the off-cycle cases. Chapter 8: HealthTrade's first three runs. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Station 1: the pre-run checklist", "12", "<p>The run computes from the records as they stand at generation. Station 1 brings the records current, on a written checklist, closed by a stated date (the records-close date — the 20th in the working shape). Walk the lines.</p><p><b>1. Joiners.</b> Every employee who started this period: Employee record verified (module 1, Chapter 2 — the seven fields, the attachments), Salary Structure Assignment created with Base from the offer letter and the slab attached, and the joiner's slip <b>previewed</b> against the letter (module 1, Chapter 7). A joiner missing any of these is excluded from the run and paid on the correction lane — worse for everyone; hence the checklist.</p><p><b>2. Leavers.</b> Every exit this period: Status and <b>Relieving Date</b> set on the Employee record, so the final slip prorates to the date and later runs exclude them (module 7 completes the exit computation — settlements, encashments; this line is the run's dependency).</p><p><b>3. Pay changes.</b> Every approved rise or adjustment effective this period: recorded as a <b>new assignment from the effective date</b> (module 1's supersession rule), previewed. The approval evidence attached — the run executes decisions; module 6 governs them.</p><p><b>4. Leave and absence.</b> Approved leave-without-pay and unapproved absences recorded in the leave machinery by the records-close date — payment-days proration (module 1, Chapter 6) reads them at generation. The month's LWP list is printed for Station 3's spot-checks: the reviewer must know which slips <b>should</b> show reduced days.</p><p><b>5. Variable pay inputs.</b> Overtime, bonuses, commissions, and one-off deductions for the period, entered through module 5's machinery (Additional Salary records) by the close date, each with its approval. The run picks them up automatically; late entries become correction-lane traffic.</p><p><b>6. The standing records unchanged.</b> A one-line confirmation: no component, structure, or slab changed since last run — or, where one did, the change ran module 8's discipline (previewed per affected structure, logged). The run should never be the place a structural change is discovered.</p><p><b>The close line.</b> At the records-close date the checklist is signed — every line confirmed or its exception named — and the inputs freeze: what arrives after the close waits for next month or takes the correction lane deliberately. The freeze is what makes Station 3's review meaningful; a review of moving records reviews nothing.</p><p>Practical rule: the checklist is a standing document with the month's date, kept in the payroll folder beside the register it will produce. Six lines, most of them one word — <i>done</i> — and the run's quality is decided here, before the Payroll Entry exists.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Station 2: generating the run", "12", "<p>Open <b>Payroll Entry</b> (awesome bar) &gt; New. Walk the form, top to bottom.</p><p><b>1. Company and Posting Date.</b> The employing entity, and the date the accrual will book to — month-end for the standard monthly cycle, so the cost lands in the month it belongs to (the Accounts track's period discipline).</p><p><b>2. Payroll Frequency and the period.</b> Monthly, with the <b>Start Date</b> and <b>End Date</b> defining the period the slips compute — the payment-days engine counts working days inside these dates.</p><p><b>3. The selection filters.</b> The run can scope by Branch, Department, Designation, or company-wide. The standing design: <b>one run per company per period</b>, unscoped, so no employee can fall between two partial runs — scoped runs are for the deliberate exception (a branch on a different cycle), recorded as a design decision, not improvised monthly.</p><p><b>4. The payable and payment defaults.</b> The payroll payable account (the net-pay liability the accrual credits — module 2, Chapter 6's map) and the payment account/mode defaults the bank entry will use. Set once, verified at the first run's posting read, untouched after.</p><p><b>5. Get Employees.</b> The button pulls every employee matching the filters with an <b>active assignment in the period</b> into the document's employee table. Read the table before going further:</p><p>• <b>The count.</b> Against the expectation: last month's count + joiners − leavers. This is the first sighting of the headcount reconciliation (Station 3 formalises it); a surprise here is cheaper than a surprise in the drafts.<br>• <b>The absences from the list.</b> A missing employee means a missing or lapsed assignment — back to Station 1's checklist, fix the record, Get Employees again. The run never proceeds around a missing person \"to be fixed later\".</p><p><b>6. Create Salary Slips.</b> The button generates one <b>draft</b> Salary Slip per listed employee, each computed from that employee's assignment, structure, components, slab, and payment days — modules 1 and 2 executing in bulk. Drafts, deliberately: nothing has posted, nothing has paid, everything is readable and correctable. Station 3 now owns them.</p><p><b>The generation log line.</b> One line in the payroll log: period, filter scope (company-wide), employee count pulled, slips created, date, by whom. The line seems trivial until the month it is not — the run that was generated twice, the scope that was accidentally filtered — and the log is where those diagnoses start.</p><p>Practical rule: generate once, after the records close — not \"early to see\" and again later. A regenerated run must first cancel its earlier drafts cleanly, and two generations in one period is exactly how a stale draft slips through to submission. One close, one generation, one review.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Station 3: the draft review", "13", "<p>The drafts exist; nothing has posted or paid. Station 3 is the read that stands between computation and money — three checks, in order, evidenced.</p><p><b>Check 1 — the headcount reconciliation.</b> One line of arithmetic, written on the checklist:</p><p><i>last month's paid count + this month's joiners − this month's leavers = this month's draft count.</i></p><p>The joiner and leaver names are listed (Station 1 produced both lists). If the equation does not balance, the difference is named <b>by person</b> before anything else happens — the joiner whose assignment lapsed, the leaver still active, the duplicate. The reconciliation is thirty seconds when clean and the single highest-value control in the run: it is the check that catches the ghost employee, the missed exit, and the double slip, which are payroll's three classic frauds and its three classic accidents alike.</p><p><b>Check 2 — the register comparison.</b> The <b>Salary Register</b> report (module 2, Chapter 8) runs on the <b>draft</b> slips, beside last month's closed register:</p><p>1. <b>Column totals first:</b> gross, each statutory column, net — each delta explained by the month's known events (headcount change, rises, variable pay, prorations). A column that moved with no story is a finding.<br>2. <b>Per-employee movements next:</b> every material change against its document — the rise's new assignment, the LWP's reduced payment days, the Additional Salary record behind a variable line. The Station 1 LWP list is ticked off here: every listed absence visible on its slip, no unlisted reductions.<br>3. The comparison is <b>before payday by design</b> — module 2 called this read's purpose: an unexplained movement found now is a record fix; found after payment it is a recovery conversation.</p><p><b>Check 3 — the spot-check slips.</b> A small set opened and read line-by-line (module 1, Chapter 7's five reads): every joiner and leaver (their prorations are the month's riskiest arithmetic), one slip per structure, and one random pick. The payment-days field on each against the calendar and the LWP list; the statutory lines against module 2's expectations.</p><p><b>The review's evidence.</b> The checklist's Station 3 block records: the reconciliation equation with its numbers, the register-comparison exceptions and their explanations, the spot-checked names, reviewer, date. Where the reviewer is a second person (the separation's stronger form — recommended wherever staffing allows), their sign-off is the block's last line.</p><p><b>Fixes found here.</b> Every finding is fixed <b>in the records</b> (module 1's rule): the assignment corrected, the leave record completed, the component repaired — then the affected draft slip is regenerated (delete the draft, recreate from the Payroll Entry, or correct-and-recompute per the document's tooling) and the relevant check re-run. Never edit a draft's figures by hand: a hand-edited draft passes review and lies to it at the same time.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Station 4: submit and the posting tie", "12", "<p>The drafts are reviewed and signed. Station 4 converts them into payroll.</p><p><b>Submitting the slips.</b> On the Payroll Entry, <b>Submit Salary Slips</b> submits every draft in the run. What submission does, precisely:</p><p>1. Each Salary Slip becomes a submitted document — docstatus 1, immutable, correctable only by cancel/amend (the platform's document law, now protecting payroll).<br>2. The run books its <b>accrual Journal Entry</b>: the posting module 2 Chapter 6 mapped — salary expenses debited by component with each employee's Payroll Cost Center, the custody payables credited (PAYE, pension, NHF at the run's withheld totals), the net-pay liability credited at the run's net total. The Payroll Entry links to the Journal Entry it created; large sites may see the posting consolidated per the entry's settings — the map is the same.</p><p><b>The posting tie — immediately, before payment.</b></p><p>1. Open the accrual Journal Entry from the Payroll Entry.<br>2. <b>View &gt; Accounting Ledger.</b> Read the rows against the register's column totals: gross to the expense lines (cost centers present on every P&amp;L row), each custody column to its payable credit, net to the payable-to-employees credit.<br>3. Tie confirmed, one line on the checklist: <i>posting tied to register, [date], [initials]</i>. A mismatch at this station is a wiring fault (module 2's diagnosis) — and it is found with the money still unmoved, which is the entire reason the tie sits <b>before</b> Station 5 rather than at month-end.</p><p><b>The point of no return, stated plainly.</b> Submission is the run's commitment: the slips are now the month's payroll of record, the books carry the cost, and the remittance obligations (module 2) now exist at these amounts. Corrections after this point follow Chapter 7's lanes — cancel/amend on documents, never quiet edits. This is why Stations 1-3 are written procedure: everything cheap happens before this button.</p><p><b>The custody sweep.</b> With the accrual booked, the custody amounts are known and owed: the sweep to the tax-reserve bank account (the Accounts track's reserve discipline; module 2, Chapter 7's funding step) is prepared now — the withheld totals moved from the operating account to the reserve, so remittance day draws on money that was set aside at the moment it became custody, not scraped together at the deadline.</p><p>Practical rule: Station 4 is one sitting — submit, tie, sweep-prepared, checklist signed — scheduled on its calendar date (the 23rd in the working shape). Splitting it across days is how a run ends up submitted-but-untied over a weekend, which is precisely the state the procedure exists to prevent.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Station 5: payment and the close", "12", "<p>The slips are submitted, the posting is tied, the reserve is funded. Station 5 moves the money and closes the month.</p><p><b>The bank entry.</b> From the Payroll Entry, <b>Make Bank Entry</b> generates the payment posting: the net-pay liability debited, the salary bank account credited — the accrual's counterpart, dated the payment date. Where the business pays through a bank upload, the payment file is produced from the run's data (employee, bank name, account number — module 1 Chapter 2's verified fields — and net amount), and the file's total must equal the run's net total <b>and</b> the bank entry's amount: one figure, three places, agreeing before release.</p><p><b>The separation, executed.</b> Module 2's rule 1 of bank governance, applied to the month's largest single payment:</p><p>1. The payroll operator <b>prepares</b>: the bank entry drafted, the payment file produced, the three-way total written on the checklist.<br>2. The <b>release authority</b> — a different person, holding the bank mandate — reviews the file against the checklist's evidence (the reconciliation equation, the tied posting, the totals) and releases.<br>3. Neither person can pay alone; the file the releaser sees is the file the checklist describes. This is the station where the ghost-employee fraud dies even if every earlier check somehow missed it: the releaser's list has names on it, and names are checkable.</p><p><b>Payment exceptions.</b> The bounced credit (a wrong account number that survived verification): the bank's return is matched to its employee, the Employee record corrected under the bank-detail custody rule (written instruction, filed), and the individual re-payment made and referenced to the run — the exception documented on the run's log, never handled by an off-books transfer.</p><p><b>The close, on the checklist's last block.</b></p><p>1. <b>The register filed:</b> the month's Salary Register produced from the submitted slips — the closed record every schedule derives from (module 2, Chapter 8's one-register rule) — into the payroll folder.<br>2. <b>The custody sweep executed:</b> the reserve transfer released (prepared at Station 4), the reserve now holding the month's statutory money.<br>3. <b>The remittance calendar armed:</b> module 2, Chapter 7 takes over — schedules derived, payments prepared, the pension window counting <b>from the payment date just made</b>.<br>4. <b>The payslip delivery triggered</b> — module 4's territory, referenced here as the close's communication line.<br>5. <b>The checklist signed and filed</b> beside the register: the run's complete evidence — six station blocks, every check's numbers, every exception's story — one document an auditor reads top to bottom.</p><p>Practical rule: the month is closed when the checklist says so, not when the money moved. A paid-but-unclosed run leaves the register unfiled, the sweep unexecuted, and the remittance clock running silently — the three loose ends that surface as module 2's four custody stories.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Corrections and the off-cycle cases", "12", "<p>Errors found after submission, and payments that cannot wait for the cycle. Both run on lanes, never on improvisation.</p><p><b>The correction lane — a submitted slip is wrong.</b></p><p>1. <b>Found before payment</b> (between Stations 4 and 5): cancel the slip (and its share of the posting per the document's cancel behaviour), fix the record at fault (module 1's rule — the assignment, the component, the leave record), regenerate and resubmit the slip, re-tie the posting, and note the correction on the checklist. The run pays right the first time; the cost was an hour.<br>2. <b>Found after payment:</b> the money has moved, so the correction is two-sided. <b>Underpayment:</b> the difference is paid promptly — an off-cycle payment referenced to the corrected slip, because the employee should never wait a month for the business's error. <b>Overpayment:</b> the recovery is agreed with the employee in writing (a deduction on the next slip through module 5's machinery, or a repayment plan for large amounts — the employee's cash flow is real, and the law and the relationship both prefer agreed recovery), documented, and executed through the records. In both directions: the record at fault is fixed so the error does not recur, and the incident line names the cause — a correction whose cause is not named will be performed again.</p><p><b>The off-cycle payment — money outside the run.</b> The lawful cases: the final settlement that cannot wait for month-end (module 7's territory), the correction payment above, the advance under module 5's advance machinery. The rules that keep off-cycle honest:</p><p>1. Every off-cycle payment has a <b>document</b> — a slip, a settlement computation, an advance record — never a bare bank transfer with a story.<br>2. Off-cycle payments post to the same accounts the map names, addressed the same way.<br>3. The month's register plus its off-cycle documents together equal the month's payroll cost — the tie extends to cover them, and the checklist's close block lists them.</p><p><b>What has no lane, stated once.</b> Editing a submitted slip's figures, paying \"outside the system\" to fix a system error, netting an overpayment against an underpayment across two employees, and holding an error quiet until next month all share one property: they make the records disagree with the money. Every lane above exists so that the records and the money never disagree — which is the whole track in one sentence.</p><p>Practical rule: corrections are logged in the payroll log with cause, lane, and dates — and the quarterly read of that log (the sysadmin track's cause-read discipline) is where recurring causes get their upstream fix: the checklist line added, the verification tightened, the record governed harder.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's first three runs", "13", "<p>The procedure, run three times — each month exercising a different station's value. (Thirty staff, three structures, the module 2 statutory build live.)</p><p><b>Run one — the full walk.</b> Records close on the 20th: the checklist's six lines signed, one exception named (a variable-pay input arriving on the 21st — held to next month, deliberately, and the submitter told why: the freeze is real). Generation on the 21st: <b>Get Employees</b> pulls thirty; <b>Create Salary Slips</b> makes thirty drafts. Station 3: the reconciliation equation reads <i>0 prior + 30 loaded − 0 = 30</i> (the go-live month's form); the draft register is compared against the old spreadsheet's final month — every delta explained, most by the corrections module 1's previews had already made; spot-checks on one slip per structure. Submit on the 23rd; the posting tie reads clean against the wiring the module 2 preview verified. Payment on the 25th: the file's total, the bank entry, and the register's net agree — the MD releases against the checklist. Close on the 26th: register filed, sweep executed, remittance calendar armed. The checklist files as the run's one-document story.</p><p><b>Run two — Station 3 earns its keep.</b> The records close clean; thirty-one drafts generate (one joiner). The register comparison flags one movement with no story: a warehouse assistant's net <b>up</b> — no rise on file, no variable line. The spot-check opens the slip: payment days show a full month, but Station 1's LWP list has him down for four unpaid days — <b>the leave record was never entered</b>. The fix runs the lane: the leave record completed in the leave machinery, the draft regenerated, the comparison re-run clean, and the incident line names the cause: the branch supervisor's leave submissions were arriving by message, not through the system. The upstream fix lands the same week — leave enters through the leave machinery by the records-close date, and the checklist's line 4 now says so explicitly. Found at draft: a record fix. Found after payday: a recovery conversation with a man who had already spent it.</p><p><b>Run three — the mid-month joiner.</b> A pharmacist joins on the 16th. Station 1 processes her fully: record verified, assignment from the 16th with Base from the letter, slab attached, <b>preview read with her offer letter open</b> — the letter quotes the monthly package; the preview shows the payment-days fraction for a 16th start, and the number is explained to her <b>at onboarding</b>, before the first payday, with the arithmetic on paper. The run generates thirty-two drafts; her slip's payment-days figure is a spot-check line; payday brings no query from her because the answer preceded the question. The payroll log's note beside the three checklists: <i>three runs, three different catches, one procedure — the stations are earning their dates.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the third law", "11", "<p>The run's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the run without a close.</b> Symptom: inputs trickling in until the hour of submission; every review reviewing a moving target; \"one more change\" as a standing state. Cause: no records-close date (Chapter 2). Fix: the close line on the calendar, the freeze enforced, late inputs to next month or the deliberate correction lane — and the review made meaningful by the stillness it reads.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the skipped reconciliation.</b> Symptom: the ghost employee paid for months; the leaver paid after leaving; the duplicate slip; each discovered by accident or audit. Cause: the headcount equation never written (Chapter 4). Fix: the one-line arithmetic on every checklist, differences named by person before anything proceeds — thirty seconds against payroll's three classic failures.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the hand-edited draft.</b> Symptom: a draft's figures adjusted directly to \"fix\" a finding; the review passing a slip whose records disagree with it; the same error recomputing next month. Cause: fixes made downstream of the fault (Chapter 4). Fix: the rule — records fixed, drafts regenerated, checks re-run; the draft is a computation's output, never an editing surface.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — pay before the tie.</b> Symptom: money released while the posting is unread; wiring faults discovered at month-end with the cash already gone; the register and the ledger telling different stories. Cause: Station 4's order broken (Chapter 5). Fix: the sequence made absolute — submit, tie, then payment — with the tie's line on the checklist as the releaser's precondition.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the single pair of hands.</b> Symptom: one person prepares, reviews, and releases the month's largest payment; every control self-attested. Cause: the separation not applied to payroll (Chapters 1, 6). Fix: the release authority distinct from the preparer, reviewing the file against the checklist's evidence — and where staffing allows, the Station 3 reviewer distinct too.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the invisible off-cycle.</b> Symptom: transfers outside the run with verbal explanations; the month's payroll cost no longer equal to its documents; recoveries that were never agreed and payments never referenced. Cause: off-cycle handled without its lane (Chapter 7). Fix: every off-cycle payment on a document, posted on the map, listed in the close block — the register plus its off-cycle documents equalling the month, always.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: name the five stations and the timing frame; run the six-line pre-run checklist and defend the records close; walk the Payroll Entry form — company, posting date, period, filters, Get Employees, Create Salary Slips — and state the one-run rule; execute the three draft-review checks with their evidence; state what submission does, run the posting tie, and place the custody sweep; execute the payment under the separation with the three-way total and run the five-line close; and route corrections and off-cycle payments on their lanes.</p><p><b>The third law: the run is a procedure — records closed, drafts reconciled and read, posting tied before payment, payment separated from preparation, and the month closed on the checklist that proves it.</b> Module 4 covers the payslip: the employee's side of everything this run produced.</p>"),
]

